course: dictionary
lesson: 597
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «Avocado and chocolate are poisonous to pet birds.» Here avocado must be a fruit, because you eat fruit and it can harm birds. Read the words around the word to decide.
explain: Avocado can also be the name of the tree the fruit grows on, or a pale green colour. Ask yourself: what would fit here? If the sentence is about picking fruit from a tree, it means the tree. If it is about painting a wall, it means the colour.
explain: Check your choice by putting the meaning into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense. If it does not, try the other meaning. Good readers do this quickly and without thinking.
fact: An avocado is a pear-shaped fruit with dark green or black skin, soft pale-green flesh and one big seed, called a stone.
fact: Avocado is also the name of the tree it grows on, and of a pale green colour.
fact: Avocados grow in warm places such as Mexico, California and Australia.
fact: We eat avocado like a vegetable, but it is a fruit, because it has a seed.
fact: Avocado is poisonous to some pets, such as birds, so keep it well away from them.
exercise: «Avocado and chocolate are poisonous to pet birds.» In this sentence, what is avocado? | A pale green colour | A kind of bird | *A fruit | A tree in a garden
exercise: She painted her bedroom avocado green. What does avocado mean here? | A tree with tall branches | A dark green seed | A fruit you can eat on toast | *A pale green colour
exercise: What is the big seed in the middle of an avocado called? | A leaf | A pip | A root | *A stone
test: The avocado tree in the garden is very tall. What does avocado mean here? | *The tree | A colour | A seed | A fruit
test: Cutting avocados is a common cause of hand cuts, so what should a child do? | Cut it quickly on the table | Use the sharpest knife and work alone | *Ask an adult to remove the stone | Leave the stone in and bite it
exam: Which sentence uses avocado to mean the fruit? | My bedroom walls are a soft avocado. | The bird sat on the avocado branch. | The avocado tree is taller than our house. | *Avocado on toast is my favourite breakfast.
